d  that will have negative repercussions  society loses and both of those  individuals lose because they have not  really self determined their fate they  are both in jobs that they don't like  if you switch them and the person who  wants to be the airline pilot becomes  the airline pilot the person who wants  to be a computer science technician  works in computer science  they both experience gains and society  is better off when people are in jobs  that they like okay that means that it  is not zero-sum there is the the total  outcome of them switching jobs is  positive alright I think we have a  general understanding of zero-sum in  nonzero-sum so now we get to the  question is social status zero-sum the  immediate question we need to answer  would be whether there is a fixed  resource is there a fixed total amount  of social status meaning that when one  person gains social status someone else  necessarily loses social status and  unfortunately as with most of these  questions it kind of comes down to how  we define social status and I know that  right now it's very popular to make  extraordinarily black-and-white claims  about the world and society but the  answer kind of depends on what you're  talking about the highest social status  like imagine a list celebrities that  might be 0 some are close to it why well  there can really only be so many a-list  celebrities because if that number grows  too much they're no longer really as  exclusively positioned as a list  celebrity celebrities because there's so  many members right so it kind of dilutes  so maybe there is kind of a limited  amount a zero-sum nature to a list  social status but think outside of  celebrity culture for example social  status is immediately much less zero-sum  imagine like reputation within  communities or smaller groups like a  small business for example a business  with 10 employees is small enough that  if one employee starts performing  extraordinarily well in the eyes of the  boss they're increased social status  doesn't necessarily correlate  lost social status for the other  employees it's sort of a different  scenario it doesn't mean that the status  of the other employees necessarily  decreases which is the definition of  zero-sum there may be some element of it  the total amount of status may fluctuate  but it's not zero-sum the the other  element by the way is sort of that  status is contextual sort of like  concentric circles you might have status  in progressive media that doesn't  necessarily confer status in other areas  so that's another reason why at the sort  of macro level it doesn't seem as though  social status is zero-sum shockingly I  know that there will be people in the  audience who will get really angry with  me and disagree and I look forward to  hearing from you about that
 

